	url = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022190273802829},
	doi = {10.1016/0022-1902(73)80282-9},
	abstract = {Solid silicon monosulfide was prepared in an amorphous form by condensing gaseous SiS at high temperature. Vaporization studies and vapor pressure data indicated that solid SiS is metastable and disproportionates into SiS2 and Si at an appreciable rate above 950°K. SiS appears to be thermodynamically unstable below 1452°K. Vaporization of solid SiS2 yields predominantly SiS and S2 species according to the dissociation process SiS2(s)=SiS(g)+12S2(g), The partial pressures of silicon monosulfide and other sulfur species were measured over the temperature range 991–1122°K and used to compute the standard free energy change of the reaction. Combination of these results and previously reported thermochemical data allowed evaluation of the thermochemical properties of solid silicon monosulfide and silicon disulfide.},
